          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does the thyroid do, and what goes wrong in hypothyroidism?",
          "answer": "The thyroid is a gland in the neck. It mainly makes a hormone called thyroxine, which the body converts into a more active form, triiodothyronine. Together these set the speed at which most tissues in the body work. This is described in the \"Hypothyroidism (underactive thyroid)\" reference.\n\nHypothyroidism means the thyroid does not make enough of this hormone. The pituitary gland, a small gland at the base of the brain, monitors the level and releases thyroid-stimulating hormone to push the thyroid to work harder. When the thyroid is failing, this signal rises before the thyroid hormone level itself drops out of range. That is why this signal hormone is usually the first blood test. The same reference says most cases are \"primary,\" meaning the thyroid gland itself is at fault. Less often, the problem is in the pituitary or hypothalamus, and the test pattern is different.\n\nThe reference I searched does not describe the symptoms of hypothyroidism, how it is diagnosed in detail, or how it is treated. Its opening section appears to continue past what was returned, so more may exist in the full document. If you have questions about your own situation, a clinician is the right person to ask.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
